WEBVTT

00:00:00.852 --> 00:00:06.181
SADL addresses a simple question: can a well regularized output still

00:00:06.196 --> 00:00:07.630
hide a degraded encoder?

00:00:07.918 --> 00:00:11.776
In joint embedding predictive architectures, the answer is yes.

00:00:13.743 --> 00:00:16.044
An encoder maps observations to H.

00:00:16.345 --> 00:00:21.019
A projector maps H to Z, where regularization is usually applied.

00:00:21.333 --> 00:00:25.368
Prediction and control depend on the distinctions the encoder retains,

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:27.932
not just the shape of the projected output.

00:00:29.951 --> 00:00:34.005
From initialization, a component shared by different inputs dominates

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:34.574
H.

00:00:34.875 --> 00:00:38.724
In a projector with training time batch normalization, adding the same

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:42.584
component to every sample leaves Z unchanged.

00:00:42.885 --> 00:00:47.061
Losses computed from Z therefore cannot correct this shared direction.

00:00:49.044 --> 00:00:54.348
SADL keeps the original objective and adds a Supply Regularizer directly

00:00:54.363 --> 00:00:55.121
on H.

00:00:55.422 --> 00:00:59.432
It reads demand from the output and measures two forms of supply:

00:00:59.835 --> 00:01:03.769
sample variation in the encoder, and its transmission to the output.

00:01:05.753 --> 00:01:08.118
The weaker supply term determines capacity.

00:01:08.431 --> 00:01:12.722
When supply is below demand, the additional loss corrects the encoder.

00:01:13.023 --> 00:01:16.421
Once supply meets demand, the penalty becomes zero.

00:01:16.735 --> 00:01:20.432
The demand is detached during this correction, so the added gradient

00:01:20.447 --> 00:01:22.148
acts directly on H.

00:01:24.116 --> 00:01:29.190
In a ten seed LeWorldModel audit with Flash attention and SIGReg, the

00:01:29.205 --> 00:01:31.966
collapse rate falls from fifty percent to zero.

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:36.984
At epoch one, sample variation rises from three point three to ninety

00:01:36.999 --> 00:01:39.657
eight point six percent of encoder energy.

00:01:41.641 --> 00:01:45.236
The control gains persist with matched attention backends and training

00:01:45.251 --> 00:01:45.893
budgets.

00:01:46.194 --> 00:01:51.447
At epoch two, INTACT with SADL reaches ninety six point three percent

00:01:51.462 --> 00:01:56.161
mean success under both MATH and Flash, across four control tasks.

00:01:58.145 --> 00:02:01.122
The same correction supports different output constraints.

00:02:01.423 --> 00:02:05.655
After one epoch, all eight tested regularizers give INTACT more than

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:08.137
ninety two percent mean control success.

00:02:08.438 --> 00:02:12.525
These rotating point clouds show actual learned representations.

00:02:12.839 --> 00:02:17.448
Separately trained glyph targets illustrate further geometric flexibility.

00:02:19.425 --> 00:02:23.728
Beyond control, SADL improves early image learning.

00:02:24.029 --> 00:02:28.045
On Imagenette, it reaches the baseline's final accuracy with about

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.147
twenty three percent fewer updates in a separate three seed comparison.

00:02:32.448 --> 00:02:35.999
On CIFAR-10, the main benefit is early in training.

00:02:37.956 --> 00:02:42.451
SADL matches what the output demands with what the encoder can retain

00:02:42.484 --> 00:02:43.357
and transmit.

00:02:43.658 --> 00:02:47.890
It adds no trainable parameters, auxiliary networks, or changes to

00:02:47.905 --> 00:02:49.160
the model architecture.
